ed through HLA differences and rejection phenomena\",\"explanation\":\"IMMUNE RESPONSE is defined as the body’s defense activity in which the immune system recognizes and responds to potential threats by distinguishing self from non-self, and HLA is highlighted as a key component guiding immune recognition. The description also notes that HLA is particularly important in transplant compatibility, because differences in HLA between donor and recipient can trigger immune rejection phenomena when transplanted tissue is identified as non-self. [Data: Entities (1775)]\\n\\nThis connection situates the community within a risk-reduction framework: determining HLA markers (via HLA typing) supports compatibility assessment to help avoid or mitigate immune recognition problems that can arise when donor tissue is perceived as non-self. The provided data does not quantify outcomes, but it clearly frames why compatibility assessment is clinically relevant. [Data: Entities (1775, 1777)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Allogeneic HCT is the primary clinical context explicitly tied to HLA typing in this community\",\"explanation\":\"The relationship data specifies that HLA TYPING is performed prior to ALLOGENEIC HEMATOPOIETIC CELL TRANSPLANT (HCT) to evaluate donor–recipient compatibility and assess whether proceeding with allogeneic HCT is feasible. This positions allogeneic HCT as the major procedural endpoint that motivates testing within this network of entities. [Data: Relationships (2955)]\\n\\nThe entity description for HLA TYPING similarly emphasizes pre-transplant use, stating it is commonly performed before allogeneic hematopoietic cell (stem cell) transplant, including allogeneic donor transplants, to evaluate donor–recipient compatibility and assess how well a potential donor matches the recipient based on HLA characteristics. [Data: Entities (1777)]\"},{\"summary\":\"The community also references AML evaluation as an additional (but less developed) usage context for HLA typing\",\"e